# Git / PR workflow (BetterGit era)

BetterGit (the owner's review app) is the only merge path. It reviews,
squash-merges, restacks, retargets, and heals. Your job is to produce
honest git shapes for it; its job is everything after.

## The one rule that prevents disasters

**A PR's GitHub base must be the branch it was actually built on.**

- Independent slice: branch from `origin/main`, PR base `main`.
- Dependent slice (needs code from a still-open slice): branch FROM that
  slice's branch and declare it: `gh pr create --base <parent-branch>`.
- Never declare `main` as the base of a branch that carries another open
  PR's commits, and never "solve" a dependency by merging a sibling branch
  into yours. Dependencies are expressed by the base, not by weaving.

Why this is non-negotiable: in 2026-07 the old rule ("all PRs target
main") plus dependent slices produced nine branches that each contained
the whole chain below them. Every PR diff inflated to ~25k lines, squash
merges resurrected a deleted module on main, and the entire stack went
CONFLICTING. BetterGit now survives that shape (it detects real structure
by ancestry, retargets, and heals), but surviving it costs healing rounds
and re-reviews. Honest bases cost nothing.

## The bleeding-edge cycle

`bleeding-edge` is the standing integration branch (main + every open PR
branch + uncommitted work in progress), so the owner's running app always
has the latest code regardless of merge status. It is a MERGE SURFACE:
feature commits never live on it directly, they live on PR branches and
reach bleeding-edge by merging those branches in (owner directive
2026-07-07).

1. Day-to-day work happens in the working tree on `bleeding-edge`,
   uncommitted while it is still taking shape.
2. The moment a self-contained piece is done (a feature, a fix round),
   commit it to a slice branch and open the PR, without being asked.
   Independent slice: branch from `origin/main`. Dependent slice: branch
   from its parent slice, PR base set to match. Follow-up work on an
   already-sliced feature is a commit on that open PR's branch, not a new
   slice. The owner expects to SEE a stack of small open PRs at all
   times; a run of feature commits that exists only on bleeding-edge is
   the failure this rule exists to prevent.
3. Merge each PR branch back into `bleeding-edge` right after pushing it
   (merge, never rebase; same for open PR tips and `main` whenever they
   move), then push `bleeding-edge`.
4. The working tree lives on `bleeding-edge`: the owner tests the running
   app from it continuously, so a tree parked on a slice branch is an app
   silently missing features. Slicing and PR fixes are one tight
   excursion (switch, commit, push, merge back, return), never a place to
   develop or verify.
5. Redeploy prod after every completed work round so the owner can test
   the live app immediately.

## Slicing discipline

- Never commit on `main`.
- One PR = one coherent group of related changes, 150-400 LOC. Meta files
  (rules, docs) ride a dedicated docs PR, not feature PRs.
- The commit message IS the PR body: motivation paragraph, `## Changes`,
  `## Test plan`. No em dashes.
- Fix review feedback in place on the PR branch (new commit + push), never
  as a fixup PR stacked on top.
- Lockfiles: commit the branch's own lockfile changes normally. If one
  conflicts during a BetterGit heal, the branch's version wins and the
  next install regenerates the rest; do not hand-edit lockfiles.

## After the owner merges: hands off, BetterGit drives

He reviews and merges through BetterGit, fast, sometimes out of order,
often while you are still slicing. BetterGit owns the post-merge
choreography: retargeting children, rebasing the remaining stack, healing
stale branches (flattening merge-woven history when a rebase cannot), and
refusing squashes that would lie. Therefore:

- Do NOT rebuild, rebase, retarget, or force-push open slice branches
  after his merges. Fetch and read the new state (`git fetch`,
  `gh pr view`), fast-forward `main`, re-merge into `bleeding-edge`, and
  continue working.
- If a slice branch moved on origin (BetterGit restacked or healed it),
  your local copy is stale: reset it to origin instead of pushing over
  it. A rewritten branch's old tip survives under `refs/cr-backup/` in
  the owner's clone if anything ever looks lost.
- If a PR you opened gets refused by BetterGit with a healing or
  containment message, do not work around it with merges or new PRs; the
  message says what it needs (usually: merge the PR below first, or let
  the heal run and the owner re-review).
